{"name":"Re-Entry","hex":"#dd484e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"re-entry","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of entering again.","The return of a spacecraft into the Earth's atmosphere."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/re-entry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Re-Entry (Big Brovaz album)","blocks":[{"text":"Re-Entry is the second album released by UK R&B/hip-hop collective Big Brovaz. After the album was delayed in May 2006, the band finally release the follow-up to \"Nu Flow\" on 9 April 2007. Following the first single, \"Yours Fatally\" and the second single \"Hangin' Around\", the third single from the album, \"Big Bro Thang\", was received a staggered download only single release in February and March 2007. The album release also contain the Booty Luv hit \"Boogie 2nite\" as a bonus track."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/65/Big_Brovaz_-_Re-Entry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Re-Entry_(Big_Brovaz_album)","wikidata":"Q7299921"},{"title":"Re-Entry (Techno Animal album)","blocks":[{"text":"Re-Entry is the second album by the illbient band Techno Animal, released on 20 June 1995 through Virgin Records. It was reissued by Relapse Records in 2023."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/96/Techno_Animal_-_Re-Entry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Re-Entry_(Techno_Animal_album)","wikidata":"Q7299922"},{"title":"Atmospheric entry","blocks":[{"text":"Atmospheric entry is the movement of an object from outer space into and through the gases of an atmosphere of a planet, dwarf planet, or natural satellite. Atmospheric entry may be uncontrolled entry, as in the entry of astronomical objects, space debris, or bolides, or it may be controlled entry of a spacecraft that can be navigated or follow a predetermined course. Methods for controlled atmospheric entry, descent, and landing of spacecraft are collectively termed as EDL."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Entry.jpg/330px-Entry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmospheric_entry","wikidata":"Q531716"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png/500px-Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png/1280px-Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png/500px-Blunt_body_reentry_shapes.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early reentry-vehicle concepts visualized in shadowgraphs of high speed wind tunnel tests","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlunt_body_reentry_shapes.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Typical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif/500px-Typical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Typical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif/500px-Typical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Typical Space Shuttle reentry profile","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATypical_Space_Shuttle_reentry_profile.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Apollo_cm.jpg/500px-Apollo_cm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Apollo_cm.jpg/1280px-Apollo_cm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Apollo_cm.jpg/500px-Apollo_cm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Apollo command module flying with the blunt end of the heat shield at a non-zero angle of attack in order to establish a lifting entry and control the landing site (artistic rendition)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AApollo_cm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Mk_2.jpg/500px-Mk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Mk_2.jpg/1280px-Mk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Mk_2.jpg/500px-Mk_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Prototype of the Mk-2 Reentry Vehicle (RV), based on blunt body theory","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMk_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Mk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg/330px-Mk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Mk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg/330px-Mk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mk-6 RV, Cold War weapon and ancestor to most of the U.S. missile entry vehicles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMk_6_reentry_vehicle_on_display_at_National_Atomic_Museum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Rv_film_pod.jpg/250px-Rv_film_pod.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c